<Base title="About" description="I'm Chiko Mukwenha, a product engineer from Zimbabwe building Hydra. I write about software, craft and figuring things out.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <p>
      I'm Chiko Mukwenha, a product engineer from Zimbabwe. I build software,
      ask a lot of questions, and write about what I learn along the way.
    </p>

    <p>
      I taught myself to code over a decade ago, starting with Drupal
      and working my way through whatever the problem in front of me demanded.
      That path took me from Harare hackathons to building products
      for organisations in London to shipping my own things in public.
    </p>

    <p>
      I care about making things well. I've written quite a bit about the boring
      work that goes into that, testing, understanding the fundamentals, and
      taking the time to ask why something works the way it does. I still think
      those things matter, even when we can get a lot more done with AI.
    </p>

    <p>
      Currently, I'm building <a href="https://usehydra.co.za" target="_blank">Hydra</a>,
      focused on helping South African businesses bring customers back.
      It gives me plenty of opportunities to discover that getting something to work is only part of the job!
    </p>

    <p>
      This blog is where I make sense of that work, and the occasional experiment
      I run on myself. You'll find software, product decisions, lessons from
      things I've tried, and thoughts that haven't quite stopped bothering me yet.
      For a more current snapshot, there's my <a href="/now/">now page</a>.
    </p>

